{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"═══ OPERATOR REQUIREMENT: warm the cache BEFORE fanning out parallel judge calls. Recorded, with the\nmeasurement that says when it binds — and why ignoring it would make parallelism a REGRESSION. ═══\n\n**MEASURED: the judge loop is SEQUENTIAL today.** From the same instrumented run:\n\n    18 overlap-judge calls, span 25.1s, mean gap 1.48s, min gap 1.23s\n\nand the code agrees — `overlap/judge.py:judge` (:117) is a plain `for cand in candidates:` (:131)\nwith no thread pool or async. So cold-start is currently handled by accident of ordering: the first\ncall writes the cache and the remaining 17 read it. **No warm-up is needed while it stays sequential.**\n\n**WHY THE REQUIREMENT MATTERS THE MOMENT IT IS PARALLELIZED — and parallelizing is tempting, because\n25.1s of wall-clock per plan review for ADVISORY output is a real latency cost.** Fan out N cold calls\nthat share a cacheable prefix and every one of them MISSES and every one of them WRITES. On Anthropic\na cache write costs MORE than ordinary input (a premium over base input price), while a read costs a\nsmall fraction of it. So the naive parallel version pays ~18 premium writes instead of 1 write + 17\ncheap reads — **strictly worse than the sequential version it replaced, and worse than no caching at\nall.** An \"optimization\" that increases cost is the failure mode to design against here.\n\n**THE SHAPE THE OPERATOR SPECIFIED, and it gets both wins:** issue ONE call first and let it complete\n(writing the shared prefix into the cache), THEN fan out the remaining N-1 in parallel, all reading.\nResult: 1 write + 17 reads for cost, and wall-clock of roughly one call plus one parallel batch instead\nof 18 serial calls. The two goals — cheap via cache, fast via parallelism — only compose in that order.\n\n**A GOTCHA IF THREADS ARE USED, which connects to story b690.** `workflow/map_fanout.py` is the\nin-repo precedent for concurrency (`ThreadPoolExecutor`), and a ContextVar is inherited by asyncio\ntasks but **NOT by raw threads**. Story b690 added per-step usage-log attribution\n(`usage_log.step_identity` -> the `step` / `model_class` columns) via a ContextVar. `map_fanout` stays\ncorrect only because the binding happens INSIDE each worker (`_map_iteration` -> `_run_leaf` binds in\nthe worker's own thread). **If overlap judging is parallelized with a thread pool and the binding is\nleft outside the pool, every judge call silently loses its `step`/`model_class` attribution** — the\nexact observability this epic just built, and the thing that made this ticket's measurements possible.\nBind inside the worker, and assert it in a test.\n\n**ADDITIONAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA**\n- [ ] if the judge loop is parallelized, the shared prefix is warmed by a COMPLETED first call before\n      any fan-out begins; asserted by a test, not by ordering that happens to work\n- [ ] the before/after comparison reports `cache_write_tokens` as well as `cache_read_tokens` and\n      input tokens — a design that turns 1 write into N writes must show up as a FAILURE, and reads\n      alone would hide it\n- [ ] if a thread pool is used, per-call `step`/`model_class` attribution survives (bind the\n      ContextVar inside the worker); asserted by reading a usage log produced by a parallel run\n- [ ] the advisory fail-safe still holds under concurrency: one judge call failing must not fail the\n      review, and must not corrupt the others' results"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785629543140477001,"uuid":"9261594c-4adf-4685-ad6c-00e10684f614"}